WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:04.680
In the blink of an eye, six glorious episodes
of The Falcon and the Winter Soldier are now

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.960
behind us.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:10.000
Let's take a look into some of the characters
we've come to know over the season, and why

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.850
they mean more than you might think.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:17.449
We first met Karli Morgenthau in Episode 1
as a masked Flag-Smasher with what appeared

00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:19.320
to be superhuman skills.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.699
She proved to be a super soldier in Episode
2, and we found out she was the leader of

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:27.929
the Flag-Smashers, a group trying to get the
world back to the way it was before the blip.

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:33.390
When it comes to Karli in the comics, well,
there's no such person — at least not directly.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:37.790
Karli was based on a comic book character
with almost an identical name — Karl Morgenthau

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.530
— aka Flag-Smasher.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:44.769
Karl's first appearance in the comics was
in "Captain America" #312, and he was a violent

00:00:44.769 --> 00:00:46.560
opponent of nationalism.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.500
Unlike Karli, Karl was not a super soldier,
only highly skilled in martial arts, especially

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.500
karate.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.570
While we could have seen a lot more from Karli,
given Karl's history in the comics, she met

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.519
her end in Episode 6 by the hands — or gun
— of Sharon Carter.

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:05.720
Unfortunately for the Flag-Smasher, super-soldiers
are not immune to bullets, and Karl's legacy

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.570
in the MCU is now over.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:12.960
We meet John Walker in the conclusion of Episode
1 as he's being introduced to the world as

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.570
the new Captain America.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.480
Fans had a hard time accepting the new Cap,
especially when he became a murderous lunatic

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:20.810
in Episode 4.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:25.700
We did get a bit of redemption for John, though:
he checks himself during Episode 6's big fight

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.420
scene, where he opts to save the GRC hostages
rather than beat up on some Flag-Smashers

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.420
a little more.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:36.130
We see him finally become U.S. Agent at the
end of the episode, officially given the title

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:40.370
by the charismatic Contessa Valentina Allegra
de la Fontaine.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:44.940
In the comics, John first appears in "Captain
America" #323.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.940
He is first introduced as Super Patriot, who
later becomes Captain America, and is eventually

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.550
named U.S. Agent — the name he now holds
in the MCU.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:58.250
U.S. Agent is more of a morally ambiguous
character in the comics, living in shades

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.380
of gray, sometimes helping our heroes and
sometimes fighting against them.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.500
He also works directly for the government,
but that doesn't appear to be the case for

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.190
the character in the MCU, since we still don't
really know what Valentina's deal is.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:17.830
He did once lead the West Coast Avengers in
"West Coast Avengers" Vol 2 #44-45, a possible

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:20.390
future role for our MCU character.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.690
The death of Lemar Hoskins is definitely one
we didn't see coming, and it was just as much

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:29.180
of a devastating blow to the viewers as it
was to John Walker.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.780
Okay, maybe not as devastating, but one of
the only people keeping Walker in line was

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.170
Lemar, who we assumed would have a solid future
in the MCU, as he's got a solid comics history

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:40.420
to work with.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:46.120
First appearing in "Captain America" #323,
Lemar was a wrestler who, like his friend,

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.510
was granted superhuman abilities by the mysterious
Power Broker.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.181
After John is dubbed the new Captain America,
Lemar becomes the new Bucky Barnes, a loyal

00:02:55.181 --> 00:02:57.300
sidekick to the so-called hero.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.140
He eventually takes on the moniker Battlestar,
a force for good who was a part of several

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:08.280
superhero teams including the Underground,
the Ducky Dozen, and Silver Sable's Wild Pack.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.780
It looks like Lemar's inclusion in Falcon
and Winter Soldier was to serve as the sacrifice

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.930
that would ultimately push Walker over the
edge, and we're now left with the thought

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:21.290
of what could have been since he was a solid
hero during his stint in the comics.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:25.470
While Battlestar might not have as complicated
a history as his friend John in the comics,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.819
he could have kept the U.S. Agent in check,
and we suspect he'll need to be kept in line

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:31.319
again in the future.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:36.120
Joaquin has been a ride-or-die ally to Sam
Wilson since Episode 1.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.650
We always suspected he was going to have a
future in the MCU given his substantial — albeit

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:44.970
relatively new — history in the comics,
and when Sam left him his old broken down

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.580
wings, we knew it was just the beginning.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.920
We only got a brief glimpse of him in Episode
6 as he listens to Sam's speech to the Senators

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.560
in the GRC, but it's definitely not the last
we'll see of him.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.810
The character was first introduced in "Captain
America: Sam Wilson" #1, as a teenager who

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:05.880
was kidnapped and experimented on with DNA
from Falcon's Redwing — an actual bird in

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:06.880
the comics.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.349
This turns Joaquin into a falcon-human hybrid,
with him eventually taking on the mantle of

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:14.370
Falcon after Sam becomes Captain America.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.269
It was clear this is where things were going
after Sam left his old wings for his new pal

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:23.300
behind, and Joaquin's comic past could hint
at him joining the MCU's rumored Young Avengers

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.300
team.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.940
Now that Sam is officially Captain America
at the end of Episode 6, it's time to name

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:29.849
his replacement as Falcon.

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:35.430
We first met Georges Batroc in Captain America:
The Winter Soldier as an adversary of Captain

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.889
America on the hostage-occupied SHIELD ship.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:42.580
The two men fought hand-to-hand, Cap tossing
his shield to the side, with Batroc coming

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.020
out on the losing end.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:48.939
He was back in all his villainous glory in
Episode 1 and reappeared in Episode 5 to give

00:04:48.939 --> 00:04:53.710
Karli and the Flag-Smashers some high-tech
weapons for their upcoming attack on the GRC.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:58.860
Batroc is only interested in killing Sam Wilson,
and gets his chance in Episode 6.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.039
He is unsuccessful, and is himself later shot
and killed by Sharon.

00:05:03.039 --> 00:05:08.220
Known as Batroc the Leaper in the comics,
the villain first appeared in "Tales of Suspense"

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.220
#75.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:14.270
Batroc is a mercenary, and former member of
the French Foreign Legion, who excels in savate,

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:18.020
a form of French kickboxing that focuses on
the feet.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:23.099
Mostly an adversary of Captain America, Batroc
has worked for HYDRA and has kidnapped Sharon

00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:25.620
Carter, and also briefly worked for Zemo.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.150
Batroc has some altruistic moments in the
comics where he actually works alongside Captain

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:34.449
America and Falcon, such as in "Tales of Suspense"
#85.

00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:38.720
However, for all intents and purposes, he's
mostly a villain.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.270
Unlike so many other characters we met in
The Falcon and the Winter Soldier, Ayo and

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:48.199
the Dora Milaje were not new to us, having
first appeared in Captain America: Civil War.

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:49.199
"Move.

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:51.370
Or you will be moved."

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:55.979
While her first appearance in the series is
calm, her second is decidedly not: she lays

00:05:55.979 --> 00:06:01.379
the smackdown on John Walker and Lemar Hoskins
with two other Dora Milaje, a group we also

00:06:01.379 --> 00:06:03.250
first saw in Black Panther.

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:07.270
In the comics, the Dora Milaje are a small
group of female warriors who are the personal

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:09.430
guardians of the current Black Panther.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:14.449
Ayo, Nakia, and Okoye have all served in the
prestigious group, though the latter two are

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:16.050
no longer involved in the comics.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:20.770
Ayo is currently a member of the Midnight
Angels, a subgroup of the Dora Milaje who

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.210
first appeared in "Doomwar" #5 in 2010.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:29.319
This group joined Deadpool in combating Victor
von Doom, a notable Fantastic Four villain

00:06:29.319 --> 00:06:32.259
who is rumored to appear in the MCU going
forward.

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:36.499
This could mean this isn't the last we see
of Ayo if the Marvel Studios writers keep

00:06:36.499 --> 00:06:39.139
with her comic history.

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:43.330
Isaiah Bradley's story in The Falcon and the
Winter Soldier is just as devastating as that

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.190
of his comics counterpart.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:48.900
Isaiah gives Sam Wilson a crash course on
his life as a soldier in the United States

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.689
Army, where he was experimented on and became
a super-soldier himself and actually fought

00:06:53.689 --> 00:06:56.240
the Winter Soldier in 1951.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.939
First appearing in "Truth: Red, White &amp; Black"
#1, Isaiah was one of 300 Black soldiers experimented

00:07:01.939 --> 00:07:07.300
on in an effort to perfect the super-soldier
serum that was a success on Steve Rogers.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:12.240
Only a handful of men survived including Isaiah,
and he eventually stole a Captain America

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.580
uniform and escaped to Europe to fight in
the war.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:20.379
He was imprisoned by the U.S. government for
his "treasonous" actions and served 17 years

00:07:20.379 --> 00:07:26.069
in solitary confinement before he was released
and pardoned by President Eisenhower.

00:07:26.069 --> 00:07:30.409
Isaiah's backstory in the comics is based
on the real-life Tuskegee Syphilis Experiment,

00:07:30.409 --> 00:07:35.830
a racist and scientifically unethical medical
study conducted by the United States government.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:41.050
It saw 600 Black men tricked into thinking
they were receiving free health care to treat,

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:45.349
quote, "bad blood," when in actuality they
were being observed to see how their syphilis

00:07:45.349 --> 00:07:48.270
would progress in an attempt to study the
disease.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:53.610
Then men who were sick with syphilis — 399
of them, as 201 men were in a control group

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:58.729
— were left to suffer without treatment,
causing many to go blind, insane, with some

00:07:58.729 --> 00:08:01.949
even dying as researchers looked on.

00:08:02.949 --> 00:08:05.900
Eli Bradley made brief appearances in three
episodes of The Falcon and the Winter Soldier

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:10.520
— when Sam Wilson and Bucky Barnes went
to meet Isaiah Bradley in Episode 2, and when

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:13.879
Sam went back to Baltimore in Episodes 5 and
6.

00:08:13.879 --> 00:08:18.119
Not much is said about Eli in the series,
as he sort of just looms in the background,

00:08:18.119 --> 00:08:21.179
but Eli's story from the comics is quite an
impressive one.

00:08:21.179 --> 00:08:25.899
There is definitely a chance we see more of
Eli moving forward in the MCU, possibly in

00:08:25.899 --> 00:08:28.339
connection with the rumored Young Avengers.

00:08:28.339 --> 00:08:33.319
First appearing in "Young Avengers" #1, Eli
is the grandson of Isaiah and Faith Bradley

00:08:33.319 --> 00:08:35.110
and the son of Sarah Gail.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:39.849
Since Sarah was born before Isaiah received
the supersoldier serum, Eli is without superhuman

00:08:39.849 --> 00:08:43.839
ability, but that doesn't stop him from becoming
a hero.

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:48.250
Eventually given the name Patriot, Eli founds
the Young Avengers, which includes Speed and

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:52.480
Wiccan, the children of Wanda Maximoff and
Vision we met in WandaVision as Tommy and

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:53.480
Billy.

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:57.680
The Young Avengers also includes Kate Bishop,
who is to be played by Hailee Steinfeld in

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.440
the upcoming Disney+ Hawkeye series.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:05.610
Just a brief appearance of Eli in "FaWS" could
mean big things for the MCU moving forward,

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.820
stoking the fire of the rumored Young Avengers
storyline that could launch in Phase 5.

00:09:11.019 --> 00:09:16.270
In a move virtually no one saw coming, Episode
5 of The Falcon and the Winter Soldier introduced

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:21.100
us to Contessa Valentina Allegra de la Fontaine,
played by Julia Louis-Dreyfus.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:25.230
"You know, it's hard, and I don't like to
repeat myself.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.580
So you can just call me Val."

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:32.190
After giving John Walker a brief talking to,
Val takes off not to be seen in the series

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:36.430
again, but we know it's not the last we'll
be seeing of her, especially since she mentions

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:38.060
she'll be calling John.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:44.210
Contessa Valentina Allegra de la Fontaine
first appears in "Strange Tales" #159 from

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:45.920
1967.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.230
Once a former sleeper agent of the Russian
Leviathan, Valentina eventually became an

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:53.089
agent of SHIELD and the lover of Nick Fury.

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:58.009
She also led the Femme Force, a group of elite
female agents who were introduced in "Captain

00:09:58.009 --> 00:10:03.050
America" #144, and were formerly led by Sharon
Carter.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:07.870
Valentina's background is complicated, with
her being a triple agent working for the Leviathan,

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:10.490
SHIELD, and HYDRA at one point.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:14.380
She has even been dubbed "Madame [MAD-ahm]
Hydra" in the comics, but her loyalties were

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:19.980
never to the famous organization, and where
her true loyalties lie remain foggy.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:24.410
Sharon Carter's presence in The Falcon and
the Winter Soldier seemed odd and many believed

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:26.090
she was the Power Broker from the get-go.

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:30.579
It turns out all the theories were true as
Sharon was revealed to be the mystery character

00:10:30.579 --> 00:10:32.949
in Episode 6 — we think.

00:10:32.949 --> 00:10:37.709
Some are suggesting she isn't, as she never
confirms Karli and Batroc's comments, so we'll

00:10:37.709 --> 00:10:40.110
just have to keep guessing until we see her
again.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:45.110
Nevertheless, the Power Broker is a Marvel
comics character with quite the rap sheet.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:50.180
First appearing in "Machine Man" #7, the Power
Broker — aka Curtiss Jackson — is the

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:54.779
leader of The Corporation and later forms
his own group: Power Broker, Inc.

00:10:54.779 --> 00:10:58.300
His business was strictly to profit from making
super-soldiers.

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:03.180
He is killed by the Punisher, another character
who might rejoin the MCU one day.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:08.269
First appearing in "Tales of Suspense" #75,
Sharon Carter has no connection to the Power

00:11:08.269 --> 00:11:09.870
Broker in the comics.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:14.130
She is a force for good but has once worked
for General Thunderbolt Ross, a character

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:17.980
many had also suspected to be the Power Broker
throughout the season.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:22.470
If these two are working together remains
to be seen, but their ties in the comics suggest

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:24.120
it's a definite possibility.

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:28.980
Ross could very well be on the other end of
the phone in Episode 6's end-credits scene.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:33.579
We have not seen the last of Baron Helmut
Zemo, and that's a fact.

00:11:33.579 --> 00:11:37.850
The last we saw of the dancing menace was
in Episode 6's finale when he was catching

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:39.889
up on some reading in his Raft cell.

00:11:39.889 --> 00:11:44.360
He seems happy to hear over his radio that
the remaining Flag-Smashers had been murdered,

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:47.149
putting an end to all super-soldiers on Earth.

00:11:47.149 --> 00:11:51.100
Interestingly enough, it was his adorable
butler Oeznik who planted the bomb inside

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:55.120
the armored car that was transporting the
remaining villains to the Raft.

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:59.060
Based on what we know of Zemo's backstory
from Captain America: Civil War and what we

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:03.139
saw in The Falcon and the Winter Soldier,
we can make some educated guesses on where

00:12:03.139 --> 00:12:05.060
his story might be heading.

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:10.970
First appearing in "Captain America" #168,
Zemo's got quite the backstory in Marvel Comics.

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:15.759
Two of his biggest roles in the comics include
his position of leader of the Masters of Evil

00:12:15.759 --> 00:12:22.279
— such as in the "Avengers" issue #273 — and
founder of the Thunderbolts, such as in the

00:12:22.279 --> 00:12:24.970
"Incredible Hulk" issue #449.

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:29.029
The Masters of Evil were a true terror for
both the Avengers and the Guardians of the

00:12:29.029 --> 00:12:30.029
Galaxy.

00:12:30.029 --> 00:12:33.480
Zemo's Thunderbolts actually housed former
Masters of Evil members.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:38.980
The Thunderbolts are a team of reformed criminals
that turned on Zemo and became heroes in "Thunderbolts

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.959
Annual" from 1997.

00:12:40.959 --> 00:12:45.449
It's possible that Zemo will create the Thunderbolts
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe, which might

00:12:45.449 --> 00:12:47.759
lead to his own downfall.

